☐ Should set CWD to the directory of the tests so that a relative path
  in the .jregr can be followed wherever Jregr was run from
☐ Handle piping of output? We do input... (" < " so should we support " > "?)
☐ Remove check for executables in -bin dir(s). Maybe this should be explicitly done for each subdir?
☐ Place any error output in the .output, if already done, why does not "<bin> not found" show up there?
☐ Remove any non-XML-compliant characters when generating XML-file

Crash handling:
☐ CommandRunner: keep the value of p.waitFor(), expose it as getExitValue()
☐ RegrCase: set fatal on signal death (exit > 128 on Unix, < 0 on Windows).
  NOT on any non-zero exit -- Alan's compiler suite is full of cases that
  legitimately exit non-zero, and that would turn 362 passing tests red
☐ RegrCase: write a diagnostic into the .output naming the signal, so a
  fatal case says why instead of being empty
☐ RegrCase: break out of the command loop, so later .jregr lines do not
  run after a crash
☐ Decide the fate of crash_should_stop_execution.expected -- status()
  short-circuits on fatal, so the file no longer affects the result
  (this group closes "Place any error output in the .output" above)

Test-suite defects:
☐ shouldCatchStderr asserts ": Fatal" and setUp() never compiles stderr,
  so the result depends on whether a stale gitignored binary happens to
  be on disk. Add compile("stderr") and assert ": Pass"
☐ AcceptanceScenarios.compile() ignores cc's exit status, so a failed
  compile silently becomes a missing binary and a misleading Fatal
☐ RegrDirectoryRecursionTest mocks java.lang.Runtime, which is final as
  of ~JDK 26. Introduce a seam (RegrDirectoryDouble already takes a
  Runtime), upgrade to Mockito 5, or pin the JDK

Build:
☐ Pin JAVA_HOME. Homebrew's ant depends on openjdk and forks JDK 26,
  while java on PATH is 21 -- so 'ant test' and a hand-run java disagree
☐ Raise -source/-target from 1.8. Deprecated on current JDKs and due to
  be removed. Nothing requires a Java 8 compatible jar -- the java8
  commits in the log are an old upgrade, not a standing constraint
☐ Narrow the 'classpath' path id from "." to "lib" -- it currently globs
  jregr.jar itself onto the compile classpath, where a stale jar can mask
  a deleted class
☐ A stale bin/ silently tests old code: javac's up-to-date check compares
  mtimes, and a fresh checkout can have sources older than old classes
☐ Release process for jregr.jar. It sat at 0.2.4 for years while the
  source said 0.2.5, and ~/bin/jregr was pointing straight at it

